<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Great Plains</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/203-bce">203 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Battle of Campi Magni · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the Great Plains was fought in 203 BCE in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medjerda">Bagradas</a> valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a> defeated a Carthaginian and Numidian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-gisco">Hasdrubal Gisco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a>, which included about 4,000 Celtiberian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> who fought to the last. The defeat led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> to recall <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-great-plains.jpg" alt="Battle of the Great Plains" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Luiz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Sulla_(loan_from_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek)_-_Glyptothek_-_Munich_-_Germany_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Scipio, who had driven the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, was elected consul in 205 BCE and given <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, with permission to cross to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. He spent about a year training his army, built around the legions formed from the survivors of Cannae, and in 204 BCE landed at Cape Farina, north of Utica, with a force that modern historians estimate at 25,000–30,000 men. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a>, the Numidian rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a>, joined him, while Syphax had been drawn to the Carthaginian side by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-gisco">Hasdrubal Gisco</a> and by his new wife, Hasdrubal's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophonisba">Sophonisba</a>.</p><p>Scipio besieged Utica but was blockaded in his camp, Castra Cornelia, by the armies of Hasdrubal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a>. During peace talks he sent junior officers disguised as slaves to study the Numidian camp, built largely of reeds and thatch. In a night attack the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> set fire to both camps and inflicted heavy casualties, and Hasdrubal fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> with 2,500 survivors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> decided to fight on with the resources at hand. Reinforced by 4,000 Iberian warriors and fresh local troops, and rejoined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a>, Hasdrubal gathered about 30,000 men within 30–50 days on a flat plain of the Bagradas River near modern Souk el Kremis, about 120 kilometres from Utica. Scipio marched there quickly without a baggage train, with a smaller army that the historian Brian Carey puts at about 20,000. After three days of skirmishing, both commanders formed up their full armies on the fourth.</p><p>Hasdrubal placed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a> in the centre, the Carthaginian infantry and cavalry on the right and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a>'s Numidians on the left. The Carthaginian cavalry broke at the first charge, and the Carthaginian and Numidian infantry, many of them survivors of Utica, fled with little resistance. The Iberians fought fiercely against the hastati, and Scipio sent the second and third ranks of each legion round in columns to strike them in the flank and rear. Enveloped, they fought to the death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Hasdrubal was demoted and exiled. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a> was pursued, defeated again outside his capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>, and captured, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> took over his kingdom. As Scipio moved his army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, within sight of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, the city opened peace negotiations and recalled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> and Mago Barca from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, but it repudiated the draft treaty once Hannibal had arrived. The war ended after Hannibal's defeat at Zama in October 202 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Great Plains” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Celtiberians served widely as mercenaries: in 211 BCE some 20,000 hired by Rome deserted Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio, contributing to the destruction of both Scipio brothers' armies, and about 4,000 fought for Carthage at the <b>Battle of the Great Plains</b> in 203 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Military</a></li></ul></section>
